{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful ruler of the County of Tripoli during the 12th century a period of significant political and military activity in the Levant. He reigned from 1183 until his death in 1203 leaving a legacy of expansion military prowess and complex political maneuvering. His reign is characterized by both successes and challenges solidifying Tripoli's position as a major power in the region.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond III was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli. He inherited the county upon his father's death in 1183. His early reign was marked by consolidating power within Tripoli and dealing with internal factions. He faced challenges from rival counts and powerful neighbors particularly the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Byzantine Empire. Raymond III skillfully navigated these conflicts establishing alliances and employing diplomacy to maintain his authority. He was known for his military acumen and his ability to raise and command effective armies.\n\nRaymond III significantly expanded the territory of the County of Tripoli. He conquered territory from the Seljuk Turks extending the county's borders further into Anatolia. He also strengthened Tripoli's defenses building fortifications and establishing a robust military infrastructure. His military campaigns were often conducted in coordination with the Kingdom of Jerusalem a crucial alliance for the defense of the Holy Land. He engaged in numerous battles against various enemies including the Seljuk Turks the Crusaders and the Fatimids.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts* Raymond III’s reign was defined by numerous military campaigns and conflicts. He actively defended Tripoli against Seljuk incursions securing vital trade routes and protecting the region from Turkish expansion. He engaged in several wars with the Kingdom of Jerusalem often playing a balancing role between the two entities.  His military campaigns extended to Syria and Anatolia where he clashed with various Turkish and Byzantine forces. He also fought against the Fatimid Caliphate securing control of territory along the Mediterranean coast. Raymond III’s military successes significantly enhanced Tripoli’s prestige and wealth.\n\nRaymond III's reign was not without its difficulties. He faced internal rebellions and political instability. His reliance on alliances often led to complex and shifting political landscapes. He also struggled to maintain control over the diverse population of Tripoli. Despite these challenges Raymond III left a lasting impact on the County of Tripoli.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1203 after a long and eventful reign. He is remembered as a strong and capable ruler who significantly expanded the territory and power of the County of Tripoli. He secured Tripoli's position as a major maritime power and a significant player in the political landscape of the Levant. His legacy is marked by military achievements diplomatic skill and a commitment to defending the interests of the county. His reign set the stage for future conflicts and transformations in the region.  He is generally regarded as one of Tripoli's most successful and influential rulers.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1805190145969391}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -4.351044481154531e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.0937630757689476}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.04358554258942604}, {"token": "▁powerful", "logprob": -0.6116936802864075}, {"token": "▁ruler", "logprob": -0.5459991693496704}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -0.8107661604881287}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.00035720644518733025}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.07556049525737762}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.0384581148